                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I love pinball games and  have purchased   every pinball game released on Steam, GOG and other outlets over the last decade as well as hard copies of some of the earlier   classics such as the thrill ride series.  I was really looking forwards to playing this one. The images and video  looked  decent enough. The classic design  and the  title also  persuaded me to buy the game. To be honest I should have looked a little closer as I was expecting at least a couple of pinball tables included in the  game, i.e. Pinball Parlour  sort of   gives you an impression of a pinball  room full of pinball machines.  Pinball FX, Zaccaria Pinball, Stern Pinball and Pinball Arcade all  give you a couple of free  pinball tables that,  in my opinion are far superior to this pinball table.  Each  extra pinball table for those Free to Play pinball games can cost as little as £1.59/$1.99 or even cheaper if you get them on sale or via one of the indie bundles (I got several  Pinball FX tables from groupees  last year  that worked out at less than  20 pence a table and each of those tables is  light years ahead of  this table).  \n\nPinball Parlour is  far too expensive for  a single pinball table and  the  actual game play is very disappointing.  I often struggled to return the ball  and more often than not  it would  glide between the  flippers   time after time  within seconds of starting a game.  I found  the ball and flipper action both felt really sluggish and the  spring loaded plunger  had no consitency. I tried using the same  position on the plungers graduated scale and then used the scale to set the plunger to different spring strengths  and  repeated the same  strengths several times for each position to see if it was possible to control where the  ball initially went before it entered the table proper. (with the aim of  lighting  up all of the channels to light up the word AGAIN, that is located at the  top of the table. It was more by chance that some were lit, there was no way to hit the ball precisely using the same plunger  scale each time. Of course, you'd expect some randomness, otherwise it would be too predictable, unfortunately there's no accuracy or skill involved playing this table.\n\nI  agree strongly with  [url=http://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561197992763618/recommended/][b]ericblr716[/b][/url] and  [url=http://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561197995230411/recommended/514570/][b]Mal's[/b][/url]  reviews.  I cannot recommend this at the present time, at least not at the asking price of £9.99/$12.99.  Sadly I will be asking for a refund, but may buy it later if I see it on sale for a minimum of 50% off or more; though  preferably I'd want to see it in one of the  indie bundles.   \n\nTo Mindware Co.,Ltd.  I do hope you are intending to either develop this table further or  add more tables to this title, I love the  look of the  table, but   the game play is not what I expected. You should check out  the free versions of the titles I've mentioned above and see how they compare. I think you'll be surprised. At the moment I think you are  asking far too much for a single  table and you also need to improve the ball physics.  Your description of the game is very misleading.",
